On the other hand, the right-hand side is $ n \cdot \binom{n-1}{k-1} $. Here, we first have $ n $ as the number of elements in the original set. Then we choose $ k-1 $ elements from the remaining $ n-1 $. This looks like a different approach. Maybe we’re first choosing a special element (the “leader” or someone we’re counting) out of the $ n $ elements. Then, we choose the remaining $ k-1 $ elements from the other $ n-1 $ elements. 

