How is this counted? First, choose the committee: $ \binom{n}{k} $, then choose a leader from the $ k $ people in the committee: $ k $. Hence, this is $ k \cdot \binom{n}{k} $. 

On the other hand, the right-hand side $ n \cdot \binom{n-1}{k-1} $ is counting the same thing but from a different perspective. First, choose the leader: there are $ n $ choices for the leader. Then, from the remaining $ n - 1 $ people, choose the remaining $ k - 1 $ members to form the committee with the leader. This gives us $ n \cdot \binom{n - 1}{k - 1} $. 

Since these are two different counting methods for the same thing (choosing a committee of size $ k $ with a leader), they must be equal. 

So this proves the identity $ k \cdot \binom{n}{k} = n \cdot \binom{n-1}{k-1} $. 

Now, moving on to the second part, which is the summation:

$$
\sum_{k=0}^{n} k \cdot \binom{n}{k} = n \cdot 2^{n - 1}
$$

